WEBVTT

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:21.050
<v Ira Glass>For so many people now, it's not a theoretical thing. It's here, this new virus. And they're having to deal with that fact. Lots of people are trying to do the right thing.
<v Ira Glass>对现在这么多人来说，这不再是理论上的事情了。它就在这里，这种新病毒。他们不得不面对这个事实。许多人都在试图做正确的事情。

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:35.370
<v Ira Glass>But it's not so clear what the right thing is-- like Luis, who works in construction in Long Island. When he got tested and learned he had coronavirus, he went back to where he was living. He shared a house with several other migrants. He's an asylum seeker, here from Central America.
<v Ira Glass>但什么是正确的事情并不那么明确——就像在长岛从事建筑工作的路易斯（Luis）。当他接受检测并得知自己感染了冠状病毒后，他回到了住处。他和另外几名移民合租一栋房子。他是一名来自中美洲的寻求庇护者。

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:50.420
<v Ira Glass>And he immediately told his landlady because she's pregnant. He didn't want to put her or anybody at risk. She said he had to leave. So he decided to isolate in the only place he had-- his car.
<v Ira Glass>他立即告诉了他的女房东，因为她怀孕了。他不想让她或任何人处于危险之中。她说他必须离开。所以他决定在唯一的容身之处——他的车里进行隔离。

00:00:50.420 --> 00:01:04.030
<v Ira Glass>He parked outside of a 7-Eleven. He didn't even want to go inside to get water because he didn't want to infect anybody. This is a voicemail message he left for the woman handling his immigration case.
<v Ira Glass>他把车停在一家 7-Eleven 便利店外。他甚至不想进去买水，因为他不想传染给任何人。这是他给负责他移民案件的女士留下的语音留言。

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.950
<v Luis>[SIGHS]
<v Luis>[叹气]

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.060
<v Luis>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Luis>[非英语语音]

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.040
<v Ira Glass>At this moment, I feel like I don't know what to do.
<v Ira Glass>此时此刻，我觉得我不知道该怎么办。

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.810
<v Luis>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Luis>[非英语语音]

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.870
<v Ira Glass>I've isolated in my car because I don't want to contaminate more people.
<v Ira Glass>我在车里进行了自我隔离，因为我不想传染更多的人。

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.260
<v Luis>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Luis>[非英语语音]

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:25.590
<v Ira Glass>I don't want people to go through what I'm going through.
<v Ira Glass>我不希望人们经历我正在经历的事情。

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:26.390
<v Luis>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Luis>[非英语语音]

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.280
<v Ira Glass>It hurts to tell the truth. It hurts.
<v Ira Glass>说出真相令人痛苦。真的非常痛苦。

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:31.300
<v Luis>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Luis>[非英语语音]

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:41.670
<v Ira Glass>You have no idea how much. He says, I haven't left my car. I've been cooped up here. If I didn't have a conscience, I'd be out there. But no, I don't have that kind of heart.
<v Ira Glass>你无法想象有多痛苦。他说，我没有离开过我的车。我一直闷在这里。如果我没有良心，我就出去溜达了。但没有，我没有那种铁石心肠。

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:42.750
<v Luis>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Luis>[非英语语音]

00:01:42.750 --> 00:02:02.220
<v Ira Glass>Zora heard this message, and she also tried to do the right thing. She found him housing to ride out the illness. Among other things, an epidemic, a plague, a contagion is a time that we're tested. And we rise to the occasion, or we don't, or we can't.
<v Ira Glass>佐拉（Zora）听到了这条信息，她也试图做正确的事。她为他找到了一个度过疾病的住所。在其他事情中，流行病、瘟疫、传染病正是考验我们的时刻。我们要么挺身而出，要么做不到，要么无能为力。

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:15.470
<v Ira Glass>Anthony Almojera very much wants his team to rise to the occasion. He's the vice president of local 3621 of the EMS Officers Union in New York City. EMS, emergency medical services-- it's part of the fire department.
<v Ira Glass>安东尼·阿尔莫杰拉（Anthony Almojera）非常希望他的团队能够挺身而出。他是纽约市急救人员工会 3621 分会的副主席。EMS，即紧急医疗服务，是消防部门的一部分。

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:30.820
<v Ira Glass>His job title is lieutenant paramedic. And besides the stuff he does for the union, he manages a crew of 50 EMS workers. Even before the coronavirus outbreak, they were short hundreds of EMS workers in the city. So they were overworked before. And it's even more so now.
<v Ira Glass>他的职位是急救医务中尉。除了工会工作外，他还管理着一个 50 人的急救人员团队。甚至在冠状病毒爆发之前，该市就缺少数百名急救人员。所以他们之前就已经工作过度了。而现在情况更是如此。

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:46.080
<v Ira Glass>Like this past Tuesday, Anthony worked a 16 hour shift at a station in Brooklyn, watching 911 calls come in. He talked to my co-worker Miki Meek. A typical day before the coronavirus outbreak was around 4,000 calls. This past week saw the first big spike.
<v Ira Glass>就像上周二，安东尼在布鲁克林的车站值了 16 小时的班，看着 911 报警电话不断打入。他与我的同事米奇·米克（Miki Meek）进行了交谈。冠状病毒爆发前，典型的一天大约有 4,000 个电话。上周出现了第一次大规模激增。

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:58.930
<v Anthony Almojera>I worked last night at 1:00 in the morning-- after we had just clocked in, 6,500 plus calls at midnight. At 1:00 in the morning, there were still 274 jobs holding. That's 274 jobs in the system that, at the moment, don't have an ambulance to get to them.
<v Anthony Almojera>我昨晚凌晨 1 点工作——我们刚打卡上班，午夜时分就有 6,500 多个电话。凌晨 1 点，仍有 274 个任务在等待处理。这意味着系统中有 274 个任务，此时此刻，没有救护车可以赶过去。

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.500
<v Mika Meek>So these are people that have called 911, but--
<v Mika Meek>所以这些人拨打了 911，但是——

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.260
<v Anthony Almojera>Right.
<v Anthony Almojera>没错。

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.250
<v Mika Meek>--no one's been able to be dispatched.
<v Mika Meek>——没有人能够被派遣过去。

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.390
<v Anthony  Almojera>Right.
<v Anthony  Almojera>没错。

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:12.100
<v Mika Meek>And how does that make you feel, when you see that you have that many call still in the queue?
<v Mika Meek>当你看到队列中还有这么多电话时，你感觉如何？

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:20.340
<v Anthony Almojera>It's a little frightening because, you know, to have been many calls holding-- just to give you some perspective-- yesterday, it was 6,500 calls-- it was the most we've ever done.
<v Anthony Almojera>这有点可怕，因为，你知道，有这么多电话在排队——给你一个参考——昨天有 6,500 个电话——这是我们有史以来处理过最多的。

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.740
<v Mika Meek>Ever?
<v Mika Meek>有史以来？

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.290
<v Anthony Almojera>Ever, yeah, even including 9/11.
<v Anthony Almojera>有史以来，是的，甚至包括 9/11 事件。

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:37.540
<v Ira Glass>Later that morning, he was in an ambulance and went on a call near Coney Island. A man had phoned in about his wife. He came home and found her on the floor. Call type said fever, cardiac arrest, COVID case.
<v Ira Glass>那天晚些时候，他在救护车上接到了康尼岛附近的一个求救电话。一名男子打电话询问他的妻子。他回家时发现她倒在地板上。呼叫类型显示为发烧、心脏骤停、新冠病例。

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:54.020
<v Anthony  Almojera>And the husband is outside. And they're both health care workers. The patient was somebody who worked in a nursing home, and the husband works in the hospital. And she was sent home Friday with fever and remained feverish throughout.
<v Anthony  Almojera>丈夫就在外面。他们俩都是医护人员。患者是在疗养院工作的，而丈夫在医院工作。她周五被送回家时有发烧症状，之后一直处于发烧状态。

00:03:54.020 --> 00:04:15.370
<v Anthony  Almojera>So I'm sitting there, talking to the husband. And he stated to me, yesterday, he called where he works to say he wanted to stay home and take care of his wife. And they told him he couldn't because he's an essential employee, that the hospital is short staffed, and he needed to come in.
<v Anthony  Almojera>所以我坐在那里，和那个丈夫交谈。他说，昨天他给工作的地方打电话，说他想留在家照顾妻子。但他们告诉他不行，因为他是重要岗位员工，医院人手不足，他必须去上班。

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:23.830
<v Anthony  Almojera>And at the moment, he judged that his wife wasn't critical. You know, she was feverish and stuff. But OK, she'll make it through the night. Then he came home and found her dead.
<v Anthony  Almojera>当时，他判断妻子的病情并不危急。你知道，她只是发烧什么的。但他想，好吧，她会熬过这一夜的。结果他回到家发现她已经死了。

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.910
<v Ira Glass>Found her dead, he's saying.
<v Ira Glass>他说发现她死在家中。

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:28.530
<v Mika  Meek>[SIGH]
<v Mika  Meek>[叹气]

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:55.730
<v Anthony Almojera>For 17 years, I've been going on these calls of cardiac arrests or other traumatic things. And it's never been an issue for me to go up to the family member when we can't do any more, and say, I'm sorry. And then I put my arm around the patient's family member. You know, I let them cry into my shoulder, I've hugged them, anything along those lines to provide a moment of empathy and sympathy.
<v Anthony Almojera>17 年来，我一直处理这些心脏骤停或其他创伤事件的电话。当我们无能为力时，走到家属身边说“我很抱歉”，对我来说从来都不是问题。然后我会搂住患者家属。你知道，我让他们靠在我的肩膀上哭泣，我拥抱过他们，任何类似的动作都能给予一点同情和安慰。

00:04:55.730 --> 00:05:09.380
<v Anthony Almojera>Yesterday, when I went outside because he happened to be outside, and I saw this man's grief in his face, and I saw him just break down and how he felt guilty about not being there, and I had to stay six feet away--
<v Anthony Almojera>昨天，当我走到外面，因为他正好在外面，我看到这个男人的脸上充满了悲伤，看到他彻底崩溃，感到没能在那里的内疚，但我必须保持六英尺的距离——

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:10.800
<v Mika Meek>Hmm.
<v Mika Meek>嗯。

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:36.670
<v Anthony Almojera>I couldn't kneel next to him like I have with other patients in the past. I couldn't put my arm around him because I'm concerned about my health because there's a high potentiality that he has it as well. And I just watched him. And it's the first time, in 17 years, that I actually got back in the truck and I cried.
<v Anthony Almojera>我不能像过去对待其他病人那样跪在他身边。我不能搂住他，因为我担心自己的健康，因为他很有可能也感染了。我只是看着他。这是 17 年来第一次，我回到车里后哭了。

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:38.660
<v Mika Meek>I'm sorry, Anthony.
<v Mika Meek>对不起，安东尼。

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:40.300
<v Anthony Almojera>No-- thank you.
<v Anthony Almojera>不——谢谢你。

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.350
<v Anthony Almojera>[DING]
<v Anthony Almojera>[叮]

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.440
<v Anthony Almojera>It's-- I'm sorry again.
<v Anthony Almojera>那是——再次抱歉。

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.370
<v Mika Meek>No, what's--
<v Mika Meek>不，是什么——

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:59.840
<v Anthony Almojera>That was the crew members. Yeah, I have about 60 messages that are unopened at the moment. Anthony, I tested positive. What do I do? Anthony, my wife tested positive. What do I do? They're all in that general nature.
<v Anthony Almojera>那是我的队员。是的，我现在有大约 60 条未读信息。安东尼，我检测呈阳性，我该怎么办？安东尼，我妻子检测呈阳性，我该怎么办？它们基本都是这种性质的。

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:10.680
<v Ira Glass>He's worried. How many of his crew won't be available soon? Going by numbers he's seen in other places, he figures half of them will end up quarantined.
<v Ira Glass>他很担心。他的团队里有多少人很快就无法工作了？根据他在其他地方看到的数据，他估计他们中有一半人最终会被隔离。

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:28.010
<v Ira Glass>Right now, in Anthony's crew of 50, six people are already out. The entire crew, all 50 of them, have been exposed to somebody with COVID-19 at this point. In the past, the policy for New York Fire Department EMS workers was, when you're exposed to a contagious virus like that, you weren't supposed to come to work.
<v Ira Glass>目前，在安东尼的 50 人团队中，已经有 6 人无法工作。整个 50 人的团队目前都已经接触过 COVID-19 患者。过去，纽约消防局 EMS 员工的政策是，当你接触到这种传染性病毒时，就不应该去上班。

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:37.780
<v Ira Glass>But the policy changed about a week and a half ago. Now you stay home only if you're actually showing symptoms. Anthony thinks it's because the city is so desperate for EMS workers right now.
<v Ira Glass>但政策在大约一周半前改变了。现在，只有当你真正表现出症状时，你才留在家中。安东尼认为这是因为该市目前太急需急救人员了。

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:46.870
<v Anthony Almojera>So if they can squeeze another five or six days out of you before you become symptomatic, I think that's what their motivation is.
<v Anthony Almojera>所以，如果他们能在你出现症状之前再压榨你五六天，我想这就是他们的动机。

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:53.160
<v Mika Meek>It's not said explicitly. But the crews are short. They need workers. You guys are essential.
<v Mika Meek>虽然没有明确说出来。但团队人手短缺。他们需要员工。你们是重要员工。

00:06:53.160 --> 00:07:02.160
<v Anthony Almojera>It feels we're expendable. Now, listen, I don't believe that chief of EMS feels we are expendable. I don't want to put that on her.
<v Anthony Almojera>感觉我们是可有可无的。听着，我不相信急救部门的主管认为我们是可有可无的。我不想把这一点归咎于她。

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:02.840
<v Mika Meek>Mm-hmm.
<v Mika Meek>嗯嗯。

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:14.060
<v Anthony  Almojera>But it feels, overall, by the department and the city that we're just going to keep putting you out there because we don't have any other options, because we didn't properly prepare for this.
<v Anthony  Almojera>但从部门和城市的整体来看，感觉我们只是会被不断地派出去，因为他们别无选择，因为我们没有为此做好充分准备。

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:38.150
<v Ira Glass>Spokesperson for the fire department told us that EMS workers are expected to show up if they've been exposed to the virus because at this point in New York, all EMS personnel have likely been exposed to the virus. Right now, EMS workers don't even have a way to get tested to see if they have the virus, just like the rest of us. And Anthony estimates they're going to run out of N95 masks in less than a week, this coming Friday, April 3.
<v Ira Glass>消防部门发言人告诉我们，急救人员如果接触过病毒，仍被要求上班，因为在纽约目前的阶段，所有急救人员都可能已经接触过病毒。现在，急救人员甚至没有检测途径来确认自己是否感染，就像我们其他人一样。安东尼估计，他们的 N95 口罩在不到一周内就会用完，即即将到来的 4 月 3 日星期五。

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:40.740
<v Mika Meek>What are you telling your workers right now?
<v Mika Meek>你现在对你的员工说了些什么？

00:07:40.740 --> 00:08:01.700
<v Anthony  Almojera>Well, the tough thing about this is we all signed up for this, you know? In a pandemic, that is EMS' time to shine, right? Treating viruses is what we do all day long. You name it, you know, HIV, hepatitis, meningitis. This is what we do.
<v Anthony  Almojera>关于这一点，最困难的是我们都签了这个协议，你知道吗？在流行病中，这是 EMS 大显身手的时刻，对吧？治疗病毒是我们整天都在做的事情。你说得出的，你知道，艾滋病、肝炎、脑膜炎。这就是我们所做的。

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:18.070
<v Anthony  Almojera>But in order to do it effectively, we have to believe that when we're out there with the potential of dying, getting hurt or seriously ill, that if something happens to us, we're going to be taken care of. And the consensus among the membership is that's not going to happen.
<v Anthony  Almojera>但为了有效地做到这一点，我们必须相信，当我们面临死亡、受伤或严重疾病的风险在外工作时，如果发生什么事，我们会得到照顾。而大家一致认为这不会发生。

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.530
<v Mika Meek>The feeling is we're not going to be taken care of.
<v Mika Meek>这种感觉就是我们不会得到照顾。

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:21.500
<v Anthony Almojera>Yes.
<v Anthony Almojera>是的。

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:24.590
<v Mika Meek>What do you think the next couple weeks are going to be like?
<v Mika Meek>你认为接下来的几周会是什么样子？

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:29.660
<v Anthony Almojera>Oh, we're not even to the halfway point here. You know how they keep talking about flattening the curve?
<v Anthony Almojera>哦，我们还没到一半呢。你知道他们一直说的“拉平曲线”吗？

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:30.220
<v Mika Meek>Mm-hmm.
<v Mika Meek>嗯嗯。

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:36.170
<v Anthony Almojera>We're on the upward tick of that curve still.
<v Anthony Almojera>我们仍处于那条曲线的上升期。

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:39.840
<v Mika Meek>Do you have any workers who are wanting to quit?
<v Mika Meek>有没有员工想要辞职？

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:43.970
<v Anthony Almojera>No, I haven't had any workers that wanted to quit. I have not heard that.
<v Anthony Almojera>没有，我没有听说有员工想要辞职。我还没听说过。

00:08:43.970 --> 00:09:05.130
<v Ira Glass>Because, like, the virus is here now. It's testing us, all of us, EMS workers, and doctors, and nurses, who are now risking their lives, but also delivery people, postal workers, politicians, those of us who are suddenly out of work, parents who have no talent for homeschooling at all, even those of us who are just on lockdown, worried for older relatives.
<v Ira Glass>因为，病毒现在就在这里。它正在考验我们所有人——急救人员、医生、护士，他们现在冒着生命危险，还有快递员、邮政工人、政客、那些突然失业的人、那些根本没有居家教育天赋的父母，甚至是我们这些仅仅处于封锁状态、担心年长亲人的人。

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:30.590
<v Ira Glass>We're all being tested. So today in our program, we're going to hear from people who are right in the middle of it now, being tested in pretty extreme ways. Plus, before the hour's out, a call from the future from somebody in China who's kind of on the other side of this thing. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们都在接受考验。所以今天在我们的节目中，我们将听到那些正处于困境中心、正以相当极端的方式接受考验的人们的心声。此外，节目结束前，还会有一个来自未来的电话，来自中国，那里的人正处于这场灾难的另一面。来自芝加哥 WBEZ，这是《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:48.870
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "STAY YOUR ASS INDOORS" BY MNEK]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - MNEK 的《STAY YOUR ASS INDOORS》]

00:09:48.870 --> 00:10:25.540
<v Ira      Glass>Throughout this episode, we're featuring original songs that we heard on Instagram created by the British musician and producer MNEK while he's been home alone and social distancing. He's jokingly calling these songs his Corona EP, and they are all him, no backing band, looping his own voice. The songs are part PSA, part pure pop, just a man bringing some light in a dark moment. Now, we turn to Act One.
<v Ira      Glass>在本集节目中，我们将播出我们在 Instagram 上听到的原创歌曲，这些歌曲是由英国音乐家兼制作人 MNEK 在独自在家保持社交距离时创作的。他开玩笑地称这些歌曲为他的“新冠 EP”，而且全都是他一个人录制的，没有伴奏乐队，循环播放他自己的声音。这些歌曲既是公共服务广告，又是纯粹的流行乐，只是一个在黑暗时刻带来一丝光明的人。现在，进入第一幕。

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:42.680
<v Ira Glass>Act One, The Inside Game-- OK, so this virus is out there. And so many of us are scared to get it and wonder what would happen if we did get it. And honestly, for all the coverage of the illness, we have not heard the people that it's happened to talk at length about that experience very much.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，室内游戏——好吧，这种病毒就在外面。我们很多人都害怕感染它，想知道如果感染了会怎样。老实说，尽管对这种疾病有大量的报道，但我们很少听到感染者详细谈论那段经历。

00:10:42.680 --> 00:11:04.170
<v Ira Glass>One thing that came up here is-- we've all talked about this-- is what is going on in the homes where both parents get the virus, like how do they manage? And just coincidentally, one of our co-workers, Ben Calhoun, has been worried about his brother-in-law, Elia Einhorn, and Elia's wife, Amy, who are in that exact situation. They were among the first wave of COVID-19 patients in New York City.
<v Ira Glass>这里出现的一件事是——我们都谈论过这个问题——那些父母双方都感染病毒的家庭现在是什么情况，他们是如何应对的？巧合的是，我们的一位同事本·卡尔霍恩（Ben Calhoun）一直很担心他的姐夫埃利亚·艾因霍恩（Elia Einhorn）和埃利亚的妻子艾米（Amy），他们正处于那种境地。他们是纽约市第一批 COVID-19 患者。

