<v Ira  Glass>[MUSIC - "QUARANTINE" BY MNEK]
<v Ira  Glass>[音乐 - MNEK 的《QUARANTINE》]

00:23:49.430 --> 00:24:10.570
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, The View From the Park-- so one of the producers here at our show, Emanuele Berry, got to know reporter Jiayang Fan back when were doing our Hong Kong show this past fall. Jiayang's a staff writer at The New Yorker. Emanuele and she have stayed in touch since then. Emanuele was the editor for a story that Jiayang did here a few weeks ago.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，公园里的风景——我们节目的制作人之一埃曼努埃尔·贝里（Emanuele Berry），在去年秋天我们制作香港节目时结识了记者贾阳·范（Jiayang Fan）。贾阳是《纽约客》的特约撰稿人。埃曼努埃尔和她从那时起一直保持联系。埃曼努埃尔是贾阳几周前在这里做的一个故事的编辑。

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:29.110
<v Ira Glass>Jiayang has a mom who is in a medical facility in New York City. Jiayang tweets about her all the time. And right now, Jiayang's in the situation that a lot of us are in, worried for our parents, but a much more extreme version of that than most of us are dealing with. Like already, so much has happened with her mom. Emanuele checked in with her.
<v Ira Glass>贾阳的母亲在纽约市的一家医疗机构。贾阳经常在推特上发关于她的内容。现在，贾阳正处于我们许多人所处的境地，担心我们的父母，但比我们大多数人处理的情况要极端得多。比如，她母亲身上已经发生了太多事情。埃曼努埃尔联系了她。

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:31.220
<v Emanuele Berry>How are you?
<v Emanuele Berry>你好吗？

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:37.750
<v Jiayang Fan>I am trying to hang in there, I guess, is the most optimistic way of putting it.
<v Jiayang Fan>我在努力坚持，我想，这是最乐观的说法。

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:55.290
<v Emanuele  Berry>Yeah, yeah. I mean, I follow you on Twitter. I see that you tweet a lot about your mom. But I don't actually exactly know what your mother's ailment is. I just know she's hospitalized. Can you-- what's going on there?
<v Emanuele  Berry>是的，是的。我的意思是，我在推特上关注你。我看到你经常发关于你母亲的推文。但我并不确切知道你母亲的疾病是什么。我只知道她住院了。你能——那里发生了什么？

00:24:55.290 --> 00:25:23.900
<v Jiayang Fan>My mother doesn't have control of any part of her body at this point, except for her eyeballs, the movement of her eyeballs. So she is completely cognitively intact and as sharp as she ever has been. But she's imprisoned in a body that refuses to cooperate in any way.
<v Jiayang Fan>我母亲目前无法控制身体的任何部位，除了她的眼球，眼球的转动。所以她在认知上完全正常，和以往一样敏锐。但她被囚禁在一个拒绝以任何方式合作的身体里。

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:53.780
<v Jiayang Fan>She hasn't left her bed, her hospital bed, except to go to the ICU for the last six years. The only time she's ever seen any sliver of the sky is in those few minutes when paramedics push her from the hospital entrance into an ambulance. So when I visit her, I feel like I'm bringing the world to her.
<v Jiayang Fan>除了去 ICU，她过去六年来没有离开过她的床，她的病床。她唯一一次看到天空碎片的时候，是在救护人员把她从医院入口推进救护车的那几分钟。所以当我去看她时，我觉得我把整个世界带给了她。

00:25:53.780 --> 00:26:08.410
<v Emanuele   Berry>Her mother, Yao Li, has ALS. She's lived in a facility for the past six years. She always has an aid by her side. Jiayang's apartment is just a three minute walk away.
<v Emanuele   Berry>她的母亲姚莉（Yao Li）患有渐冻症（ALS）。她在过去六年里一直住在一个设施里。她身边总有一名护理人员。贾阳的公寓离那只有三分钟的步行路程。

00:26:08.410 --> 00:26:24.160
<v Emanuele   Berry>She visits every day, sometimes twice a day, unless she's traveling for work. When Jiayang visits, they talk. Well, mostly Jiayang talks. Yao Li spells things out using her eyes and an alphabet chart. It's slow. But that doesn't stop Yao Li from expressing herself.
<v Emanuele   Berry>她每天都去探望，有时一天两次，除非她因工作出差。当贾阳去探望时，他们交谈。好吧，主要是贾阳在说。姚莉用她的眼睛和字母表拼写东西。这很慢。但这并没有阻止姚莉表达自己。

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:36.000
<v Jiayang Fan>Sometimes she tries to tell me bawdy jokes over the alphabet chart that she spells with her eyes. And it'll take me an hour. And I'll think that she's requesting--
<v Jiayang Fan>有时她试着通过字母表告诉我一些下流的笑话，她用眼睛拼写出来。这会花掉我一个小时。我会以为她在要求——

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:36.770
<v Emanuele Berry>[LAUGHS]
<v Emanuele Berry>[笑声]

00:26:36.770 --> 00:26:47.270
<v Jiayang Fan>--a medication or that some part of her-- like she'll mention butt, and I'll be like, oh, is her butt hurting? You know, what-- do you have an infection? And it's she's trying to tell me a butt joke. So that's--
<v Jiayang Fan>——药物，或者她身体的某一部分——比如她会提到臀部，我会说，哦，是她的臀部在痛吗？你知道，什么——你有感染吗？结果她是在试着给我讲一个臀部笑话。所以这就是——

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:48.570
<v Emanuele Berry>[LAUGHS]
<v Emanuele Berry>[笑声]

00:26:48.570 --> 00:27:18.130
<v Jiayang  Fan>So-- so that gives you a little bit of a sense of my mom. She is someone who [LAUGHS] who is relentlessly truthful. Whenever a piece comes out of mine, her first question is, read me all the negative tweets about it. What is the criticism like? What are your trolls sayings?
<v Jiayang  Fan>所以——这让你对我妈妈有个了解。她是一个（笑声）无情地诚实的人。每当我的一篇文章发表，她的第一个问题就是，给我读读所有关于它的负面推文。批评是什么样的？你的喷子们是怎么说的？

00:27:18.130 --> 00:27:32.430
<v Jiayang  Fan>And I know that sounds absurd and funny. But I think that comes from an immigrant's survival mentality. She wants to know what I need to prepare myself for in order to survive.
<v Jiayang  Fan>我知道这听起来很荒谬和有趣。但我认为这来自于移民的生存心态。她想知道我需要为生存做些什么准备。

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:41.440
<v Emanuele  Berry>So what happened last week? Take me through what happened last week.
<v Emanuele  Berry>那么上周发生了什么？带我回顾一下上周发生的事情。

00:27:41.440 --> 00:28:00.020
<v Jiayang Fan>Remember, my mother is always-- in the best of times, my mother is a mild infection away from death, in the best of times. The doctors have told me, again and again, she will die of pneumonia. It's just a matter of time.
<v Jiayang Fan>记住，我母亲总是——即使在最好的时候，我母亲距离死亡也仅一步之遥。医生一次又一次地告诉我，她会死于肺炎。这只是时间问题。

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:13.840
<v Jiayang Fan>She's already on a respirator, a machine that breathes for her. There's not that much that can be done. So when I hear news about viruses entering nursing homes, every nerve in my body is on full alarm.
<v Jiayang Fan>她已经在用呼吸机了，那是一种为她呼吸的机器。能做的并不多。所以当我听到有关病毒进入疗养院的消息时，我全身的每一根神经都处于全面警惕状态。

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:28.730
<v Emanuele Berry>Jiayang once told me that her mother lives for her. But the reverse is also true. Jiayang lives for her mom. They're each other's only family, which means Jiayang is a very active family member at her mom's facility.
<v Emanuele Berry>贾阳曾告诉我，她母亲是为了她而活。但反过来也一样。贾阳是为了她妈妈而活。她们是彼此唯一的家人，这意味着贾阳是她母亲所在机构里非常积极的家庭成员。

00:28:28.730 --> 00:28:48.890
<v Emanuele Berry>The staff knows her. They know how protective she is. So in early March, when she heard about the virus spreading in nursing homes, she called, said, hey, I know it's a stressful time. But could you ask doctors and nurses to wear gloves and masks before getting too close to my mom? She says she could hear the tone in her own voice, anxious and demanding.
<v Emanuele Berry>工作人员认识她。他们知道她有多么保护她。所以在三月初，当她听说病毒在疗养院蔓延时，她打电话说，嘿，我知道现在是很紧张的时候。但能不能请医生和护士在靠近我妈妈之前戴上手套和口罩？她说她能听出自己声音中的语气，焦虑而苛刻。

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:57.390
<v Jiayang Fan>This is not a tone I've ever taken with any member of my mom's facility for as long as she'd been there. But I was-- I was unnerved enough--
<v Jiayang Fan>只要我妈妈在那里，我就从来没用这种语气和任何工作人员说过话。但我当时——但我被吓到了——

00:28:57.390 --> 00:28:58.760
<v Emanuele Berry>You were freaked out.
<v Emanuele Berry>你吓坏了。

00:28:58.760 --> 00:28:59.750
<v Jiayang Fan>Yeah.
<v Jiayang Fan>是的。

00:28:59.750 --> 00:29:00.730
<v Emanuele Berry>Yeah.
<v Emanuele Berry>是的。

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:19.200
<v Jiayang  Fan>I was unnerved enough about what I was reading that I called first thing in the morning to say that. And I think the staff who picked up, was very annoyed with me and just said-- and basically said, I can't do that. Like, I can't-- I don't-- I can't tell other people here what to do.
<v Jiayang  Fan>我被我读到的东西吓坏了，所以一大早我就打电话说了那番话。我想接电话的工作人员对我非常恼火，只是说——基本上说，我做不到。就像，我不能——我不——我不能告诉这里的其他人该怎么做。

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:38.030
<v Jiayang  Fan>And then I think she thought she hung up. She didn't actually hang up. And she said, yeah, Yao Li's daughter just called. And she thinks that, like, we have to wear gloves and, you know, masks like around every patient at the hospital. And there was-- I heard laughter all around.
<v Jiayang  Fan>然后我觉得她以为她挂断了电话。她其实没挂。她说，是的，姚莉的女儿刚打了电话。她认为，我们必须在医院里的每个病人身边戴上手套和，你知道，口罩。然后我听到周围一片笑声。

00:29:38.030 --> 00:29:48.270
<v Jiayang  Fan>And then, really, unfortunately, I heard her say-- I still, to this day, do not know who that staff member was. But she said, you know, and they're the ones who are Chinese.
<v Jiayang  Fan>然后，真的很不幸，我听到她说——直到今天，我仍然不知道那个工作人员是谁。但她说，你知道，而且她们还是中国人。

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:59.320
<v Jiayang  Fan>To be frank, I wasn't offended. I wasn't like, oh, my God. This is so racist. I was just worried. I was worried about having offended her. I was worried about offending people who are the first line of defense.
<v Jiayang  Fan>坦白说，我并没有被冒犯。我没有那种，哦，天哪。这太种族主义了。我只是担心。我担心冒犯了她。我担心冒犯那些处于第一道防线的人。

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:13.160
<v Emanuele Berry>So she put her coat on over her pajamas and ran over to the facility. But when she got there, there were security guards up front and big signs. Jiayang was so flustered she doesn't actually remember exactly what the sign said.
<v Emanuele Berry>于是她穿着睡衣披上外套，跑到了那家机构。但当她到达那里时，前面有保安和大牌子。贾阳太慌乱了，她其实不记得牌子上具体写了什么。

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:21.400
<v Emanuele Berry>But the message was clear. She wasn't getting in to see her mom. How could she be sure her mom was OK if she couldn't see her?
<v Emanuele Berry>但信息很明确。她进不去探望她妈妈。如果她见不到她，她怎么能确定她妈妈没事呢？

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:40.600
<v Jiayang Fan>I became crazed. I was calling my best friend, who were telling me that I wasn't being a rational, functional person. I had a dream about moving my mother to a different planet. I know that sounds absurd.
<v Jiayang Fan>我变得疯狂了。我给最好的朋友打电话，她们告诉我我不理智，不正常了。我做了一个梦，梦到把我妈妈转移到另一个星球。我知道这听起来很荒谬。

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:50.730
<v Jiayang Fan>And it's my subconscious-- I mean, it's my unconscious. So I'm not totally responsible for it. But I think some part of me knew that there was no corner on earth that was safe.
<v Jiayang Fan>这是我的潜意识——我的意思是，这是我的无意识。所以我对此并不完全负责。但我认为我的一部分知道地球上没有一个角落是安全的。

00:30:50.730 --> 00:31:12.350
<v Jiayang Fan>How would that even-- how would that even work with a move? She is on a feeding tube. She's on a ventilator. She's hooked up on so many different whirling machines. In the best of times, a move would be-- would require six people, not including the transport.
<v Jiayang Fan>那种转移会怎么——怎么运作？她靠饲管维持。她戴着呼吸机。她连接着这么多台不同的旋转机器。在最好的时候，转移也需要六个人，还不算运输人员。

00:31:12.350 --> 00:31:29.480
<v Emanuele Berry>Talking to Jiayang, I noticed she couldn't help herself going down these thought spirals, far fetched ways to save her mom, all the things that could go wrong. And I think that's because she can't do anything, and she wants to badly. But there isn't a way out.
<v Emanuele Berry>和贾阳交谈时，我注意到她无法控制自己陷入这些思维漩涡中，那些远得离谱的救妈妈的方法，所有可能出错的事情。我想这是因为她无能为力，而她又太想帮忙了。但没有出路。

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:45.610
<v Jiayang Fan>Where could she possibly go? Not in my apartment, my tiny, almost studio-sized apartment would not be able to fit all the equipment that she needs. And I wouldn't be-- I would not be fit to care for her by myself.
<v Jiayang Fan>她能去哪里？我的公寓不行，我狭小的、几乎是单间大小的公寓无法容纳她需要的所有设备。而且我自己也——我无法独自照顾她。

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:52.080
<v Jiayang Fan>Then what other facility is safe? Like, what other facility in New York City is safe? What other facility in America is safe?
<v Jiayang Fan>那么还有哪个设施是安全的？比如，纽约市还有哪个设施是安全的？美国还有哪个设施是安全的？

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:59.440
<v Emanuele Berry>And so you're basically at a point where you're like there-- I can't-- there's nothing I can-- there's nothing I can do.
<v Emanuele Berry>所以你基本上到了一个地步，你觉得——我不能——我做不了什么。

00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:26.650
<v Jiayang Fan>I'm basically at the point where my unconscious is dreaming up plants to take my mother to Mars. My mind goes to very strange places, where I think about being told that my mother's ventilator has to be-- that my mother's ventilator is better--
<v Jiayang Fan>我基本上到了我的潜意识在梦想着带我母亲去火星的地步。我的思绪去了非常奇怪的地方，我想到被告知我母亲的呼吸机必须——我母亲的呼吸机更适合——

00:32:26.650 --> 00:32:35.740
<v Jiayang Fan>I'm sorry. It's hard for me to even come up the grammar for this because it's so hard. That my mother's ventilator could better serve someone who's younger and healthier.
<v Jiayang Fan>对不起。我甚至很难组织语言，因为它太难了。我母亲的呼吸机可能更适合那些更年轻、更健康的人。

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:44.510
<v Emanuele Berry>This hasn't happened. Nobody's suggested her mom give up her ventilator. But she can't stop thinking of the possibility of it.
<v Emanuele Berry>这还没发生。没人建议她妈妈放弃呼吸机。但她无法停止思考这种可能性。

00:32:44.510 --> 00:33:07.160
<v Jiayang Fan>And then my mind goes down a rabbit hole. I start looking up surgeries where I could give up half my lung, maybe, so that this healthy person can still be saved because-- because it's about lungs filling with fluids, right? And I think, well, if I could save that other person, then my mother gets to keep her ventilator.
<v Jiayang Fan>然后我的思绪钻进了牛角尖。我开始查找手术，我可以捐出一半肺，也许，这样这个健康的人就能被拯救，因为——因为这是关于肺部充满液体，对吧？我想，嗯，如果我能救那个人，那么我母亲就能保留她的呼吸机。

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:23.970
<v Jiayang Fan>And your mind just spends hours in those rabbit holes, which are completely-- it's completely useless, right? Because it's not-- I mean, these are not my decisions. What I'm suggesting is probably totally insane and scientifically, medically impractical.
<v Jiayang Fan>你的大脑只是花了几小时在那些牛角尖里，这完全是——这完全是无用的，对吧？因为这不是——我的意思是，这不是我的决定。我所建议的可能完全是疯狂的，在科学和医学上是不切实际的。

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:31.220
<v Emanuele Berry>Jiayang did get a small bit of relief. She managed to get in for one last visit. That was two weeks ago.
<v Emanuele Berry>贾阳确实得到了一点缓解。她设法进行了最后一次探望。那是两周前的事了。

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:35.490
<v Emanuele Berry>What did you guys say to each other?
<v Emanuele Berry>你们说了些什么？

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:55.390
<v Jiayang Fan>Because I am her daughter, I'm her caregiver. I am her conduit to the world. I have a responsibility to soothe her panic, not to stoke it.
<v Jiayang Fan>因为我是她的女儿，我是她的照顾者。我是她与世界沟通的管道。我有责任平复她的恐慌，而不是煽动它。

00:33:55.390 --> 00:34:28.660
<v Jiayang Fan>I said things to her that I think I would've wanted someone else to say to me, which is this is going to be OK. We will get through this. (VOICE CRACKING) I will see you again very soon. I don't know when, but it's going to be very soon. And we will get through this together. But they weren't words that I necessarily believed if I were to be totally honest.
<v Jiayang Fan>我对她说了那些我觉得如果有人对我这么说会很想听的话，那就是一切都会好起来的。我们能挺过去的。（声音哽咽）我会很快再见到你的。我不知道是什么时候，但会很快。我们会一起挺过去的。但如果诚实地说，这些话连我自己都不太相信。

00:34:28.660 --> 00:34:46.140
<v Emanuele Berry>She talked with senior hospital staff. And they told her there's a significant chance that coronavirus will enter the facility, that she should be mentally prepared for what that means. Jiayang thought about staying with her mom, just never leaving that visit, becoming her mom's caretaker.
<v Emanuele Berry>她和医院的高层人员谈过。他们告诉她，冠状病毒进入该设施的可能性很大，她应该在心理上做好这意味着什么的准备。贾阳想过留下来陪她妈妈，就在那次探望中永远不走，成为她妈妈的照顾者。

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:57.770
<v Emanuele Berry>But her mother's longtime aide convinced Jiayang she'd be more help to her mom outside and promised that she would stay with Yao Li, as long as she could manage. At the time, they were hopeful it would only be a few weeks.
<v Emanuele Berry>但她母亲的长年助理说服了贾阳，说她在外面对自己母亲更有帮助，并承诺只要她能撑得住，就会一直待在姚莉身边。当时，他们还希望能坚持几周。

00:34:57.770 --> 00:35:13.690
<v Jiayang Fan>(VOICE CRACKING) I mean, I doubt myself. I don't know if I made the right decision. I think that, if my mom is going to-- if she's not going to survive the illness, then I should be the one in there. And then, I mean-- and then my mind goes to dark places.
<v Jiayang Fan>（声音哽咽）我的意思是，我质疑自己。我不知道我是否做出了正确的决定。我想，如果我妈妈要——如果她不能从这种疾病中幸存下来，那么我应该是在里面的人。然后，我的意思是——然后我的思绪去了阴暗的地方。

00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:24.160
<v Jiayang Fan>I think, well, who cares if I don't pay the rent? Who cares if just everything else goes into absolute madness? All I want to be is with my mom.
<v Jiayang Fan>我想，嗯，谁在乎我不付房租？谁在乎其他一切是否陷入绝对的疯狂？我唯一想做的就是和我妈妈在一起。

00:35:24.160 --> 00:35:31.520
<v Emanuele Berry>Yeah, I mean, what do you think your mom would want you to do in this situation?
<v Emanuele Berry>是的，我的意思是，你认为你妈妈在这种情况下会让你做什么？

00:35:31.520 --> 00:36:20.730
<v Jiayang   Fan>It was-- it was really hard to leave her. She began crying, just hysterically crying, because I think she knew that, despite my assurances, that it would be a long time before I saw her again. And I think-- I think she would-- I think she would want me to stay safe. I think she would want me to find a way of coping through this crisis without succumbing to it, without being crushed by it.
<v Jiayang   Fan>离开她真的很难。她开始歇斯底里地哭泣，因为我想她知道，尽管有我的保证，离我再次见到她还需要很长时间。而且我想——我想她会——我想她会希望我保持安全。我想她会希望我能找到一种方法来应对这场危机，而不是屈服于它，而不是被它压垮。

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:56.010
<v Emanuele           Berry>At the end of February, Jiayang did a story for this show about a Chinese man reporting on the virus in Wuhan. In one of the videos this journalist makes, he introduces us to this guy he calls Ah Ming. His father recently died because of coronavirus. Ah Ming talks about being with his father in the last hours of his life, about holding his hand as his heartbeat drops to zero. For Jiayang, it was one of the most emotional moments in this story for obvious reasons.
<v Emanuele           Berry>二月底，贾阳为我们节目做了一个关于一名中国男子在武汉报道病毒的故事。在这位记者制作的视频中，他向我们介绍了这个他称之为阿明的人。他的父亲最近因冠状病毒去世。阿明谈到了在父亲生命的最后几个小时陪伴着他，谈到了当心跳降至零时握着他的手。对贾阳来说，由于显而易见的原因，这是这个故事中最感人的时刻之一。

00:36:56.010 --> 00:37:37.790
<v Jiayang Fan>His story was one that I thought just contained such pain and terror. That was unimaginable to me at the time. And now, I realize that last Tuesday might be the last time I-- I-- last Tuesday might be the last time I will ever-- I mean, that might have been the last time I saw my mother.
<v Jiayang Fan>他的故事是我认为包含了如此多的痛苦和恐怖的故事。在当时，那对我来说是不可想象的。而现在，我意识到上周二可能是我最后一次——我——上周二可能是我最后一次——我的意思是，那可能是我最后一次见到我母亲。

00:37:37.790 --> 00:37:57.560
<v Jiayang Fan>I can't even get the tenses straight. The grammar doesn't compute in my mind. And I thought that Ah Ming's experience was absolutely the most devastating. And then I think about how lucky Ah Ming was.
<v Jiayang Fan>我甚至无法准确把握时态。我的脑子里逻辑不通。我原以为阿明的经历绝对是最令人悲痛的。然后我想到阿明是多么幸运。

00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:42.080
<v Jiayang Fan>I know that if my mother contracts COVID-19, she will almost certainly die. And I-- and I also know that-- I also know that, when I get the call from the hospital letting me know that she's been infected, I won't be permitted to go in there. And I can see my mother's window every day. There's a little park that separates my apartment building from my mom's hospital.
<v Jiayang Fan>我知道如果我母亲感染了 COVID-19，她几乎肯定会死。我——我也知道——我也知道，当我接到医院通知我她已被感染的电话时，我将不被允许进去。我可以每天看到我母亲的窗户。有一个小公园把我的公寓楼和我妈妈的医院隔开了。

00:38:42.080 --> 00:39:36.050
<v Jiayang Fan>And when I stand in the park, I can see her window. I can almost see the orchid that me and my friend brought her a couple of weeks ago. Or at least, I think I can see it. And I think about-- I think about the fact that, when she is dying, if she contracts the virus, those last hours that Ah Ming held his father's hand, maybe-- that maybe I'll be standing in the park, just looking up at her window. Maybe that's the closest-- maybe that's the closest I'll be able to get to her in her last hours.
<v Jiayang Fan>当我站在公园里时，我能看到她的窗户。我几乎能看到我和朋友几周前带给她的那盆兰花。或者至少，我觉得我能看到它。我想到——我想到这样一个事实，当她垂死时，如果她感染了病毒，阿明握着父亲手的最后几个小时，也许——也许我只会站在公园里，望着她的窗户。也许那是我在最后的时刻能离她最近的地方了。

00:39:36.050 --> 00:39:47.830
<v Ira                 Glass>Emanuele Berry is a producer on our show.
<v Ira                 Glass>埃曼努埃尔·贝里是我们节目的制作人。

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:56.110
<v Ira                 Glass>Coming up, a tiny, tiny virus breaks open a jail cell. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira                 Glass>接下来的节目，一种极小极小的病毒冲破了一间牢房。稍后回来，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:39:56.110 --> 00:40:10.610
<v Ira Glass>This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, the test-- the coronavirus is here. There are people who, right now, are forced to deal with it. We hear stories of how they face that challenge. We've arrived at act three of our show.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，考验——冠状病毒就在这里。有些人现在被迫面对它。我们听听他们如何面对这一挑战。我们已经到了节目的第三幕。

00:40:10.610 --> 00:40:14.140
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Outbreak Breakout.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，疫情爆发与释放。

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:41.440
<v Ira Glass>So another way that the coronavirus outbreak has had a huge impact on people's lives-- prisons and jails around the country are starting to release inmates they wouldn't have otherwise, or wouldn't have released as early, anyway, to try to protect prisoners behind bars from getting sick. Correctional institutions, as you can imagine, are Petri dishes of germs and microbes. The latest number that I'm seeing, as I'm recording this, more than 50 inmates at Rikers Island in New York City-- that's just one jail here-- correctional officers there and elsewhere also have the virus now.
<v Ira Glass>所以冠状病毒疫情对人们生活产生巨大影响的另一种方式——全国各地的监狱开始释放那些本来不会释放，或者无论如何都不会这么早释放的囚犯，试图保护囚犯不生病。正如你可以想象的，惩教机构是细菌和微生物的培养皿。据我录制时看到的最新数字，纽约市瑞克斯岛（Rikers Island）——仅仅是这里的一个监狱——就有超过 50 名囚犯确诊，那里的惩教人员和其他地方的工作人员现在也感染了病毒。

00:40:41.440 --> 00:41:05.390
<v Ira Glass>So the city is letting 300 inmates go. Right next door, New Jersey ordered 1000 inmates to be released this past week. You're seeing similar decisions like that-- they're not as widespread-- in Ohio, Illinois, Oklahoma, California, and Texas since the crisis hit. In San Francisco, the public defender's office urged the release of, quote, "All people who are immunocompromised or over the age of 60." Producer Sean Cole talked with somebody who made that cut.
<v Ira Glass>所以该市正在释放 300 名囚犯。隔壁的新泽西州上周下令释放 1000 名囚犯。你在俄亥俄州、伊利诺伊州、俄克拉荷马州、加利福尼亚州和德克萨斯州都能看到类似的决定——尽管并不那么普遍。在旧金山，公设辩护人办公室敦促释放所有“免疫力受损或 60 岁以上的人”。制作人肖恩·科尔（Sean Cole）采访了一位符合条件的人。

00:41:05.390 --> 00:41:25.340
<v Sean  Cole>They're really two factors under consideration when figuring out who to release from jail or prison amid the COVID-19 outbreak. One, how severe was the crime? And then, two, how's the person's health? How vulnerable are they? And given these two criteria, one of the best candidates available for release--
<v Sean  Cole>在 COVID-19 疫情期间，计算谁该从监狱释放时，实际上有两个因素在考虑。第一，犯罪严重程度如何？第二，个人的健康状况如何？他们有多脆弱？鉴于这两个标准，目前可供释放的最佳候选人之一——

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:25.910
<v Terry Smith>Hello.
<v Terry Smith>你好。

00:41:25.910 --> 00:41:26.480
<v Sean Cole>Hi, Terry?
<v Sean Cole>嘿，特里（Terry）吗？

00:41:26.480 --> 00:41:27.350
<v Terry Smith>Yes, sir.
<v Terry Smith>是的，先生。

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:29.550
<v Sean Cole>--was Terry Smith. Hi, how are you?
<v Sean Cole>——是特里·史密斯。嘿，你好吗？

00:41:29.550 --> 00:41:30.310
<v Terry Smith>--or Ma'am.
<v Terry Smith>——或者是女士。

00:41:30.310 --> 00:41:31.440
<v Sean Cole>It's-- [LAUGHS] it's sir.
<v Sean Cole>是——（笑声）是先生。

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:32.510
<v Terry Smith>It's lady?
<v Terry Smith>是女士？

00:41:32.510 --> 00:41:33.070
<v Sean Cole>[LAUGHS]
<v Sean Cole>[笑声]

00:41:33.070 --> 00:41:35.390
<v Terry Smith>It's sir? OK, yeah, because I-- can you still hear me?
<v Terry Smith>是先生吗？好吧，是的，因为我——你还能听到我说话吗？

00:41:35.390 --> 00:41:37.340
<v Sean Cole>I can hear you, yeah. Can you hear me?
<v Sean Cole>我能听到你，是的。你能听到我吗？

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:39.760
<v Terry Smith>OK, I'm trying to get some privacy. I'm going to a spot where--
<v Terry Smith>好，我正试图找个私密的地方。我要去一个地方——

00:41:39.760 --> 00:41:53.950
<v Sean Cole>I got a hold of Terry at a residential treatment program for veterans called Fresh Start. And they have facilities all over the country. This one, again, is in San Francisco. The court sent him there last week when it became clear that he might be in danger of sitting in a jail cell.
<v Sean Cole>我在一个叫“新鲜开始”（Fresh Start）的退伍军人寄宿治疗项目中联系到了特里。他们在全国各地都有设施。这个，再说一次，在旧金山。当清楚他可能面临坐在牢房里的危险时，法院上周把他送到了那里。

00:41:53.950 --> 00:42:04.350
<v Sean Cole>He'd been in the county jail for nearly a year, charged with breaking into a couple of garages, nothing violent. This was just his latest stint on these charges. He's been in and out a few times.
<v Sean Cole>他已经在县监狱待了近一年，被控撬入几间车库，没有暴力行为。这只是他因为这些指控而受到的最新一次关押。他进进出出好几次了。

00:42:04.350 --> 00:42:28.790
<v Sean Cole>And at 64, soon to be 65 years old, Terry has a whole pileup of comorbidities that might sound a little overwhelming when I list them. He has a seizure disorder, PTSD from serving in Vietnam, and also being abused when he was a foster kid. He's a recovering heroin addict. And most pressingly right now, Terry suffers from chronic obstructive pulmonary disorder, COPD.
<v Sean Cole>64 岁，快 65 岁了，特里有一堆合并症，当我列出它们时，听起来可能有点令人不知所措。他有癫痫症、在越南服役留下的创伤后应激障碍（PTSD），还有寄养期间被虐待的经历。他是一名正在康复的海洛因成瘾者。而且现在最紧迫的是，特里患有慢性阻塞性肺病，即 COPD。

00:42:28.790 --> 00:42:43.390
<v Sean Cole>It's a lung disease that already sent him to the hospital a couple of times, which is why he got so frightened when he heard about the thing that's apparently coming for everybody's lungs. Inmates at San Francisco County jail are allowed to watch TV from 7:00 to 8:00 in the morning. So he just saw it on the news.
<v Sean Cole>这是一种肺部疾病，已经让他几次住进医院，这就是为什么当他听到那种显然是冲着每个人的肺部而来的病毒时，他会如此害怕。旧金山县监狱的囚犯被允许在早上 7 点到 8 点看电视。所以他就在新闻上看到了。

00:42:43.390 --> 00:42:51.050
<v Terry Smith>We watch everyday. And we saw it get worse and worse. And people started panicking in the jail. And they don't want to panic in jail.
<v Terry Smith>我们每天都看。我们看到情况越来越糟。监狱里的人开始恐慌。他们不想在监狱里恐慌。

00:42:51.050 --> 00:42:59.850
<v Terry Smith>You get a panic in jail, you know what that'd do? Where does that get you? It leads to people, oh, no. We ain't going back to our bunks. We're not-- for three days, we did not eat any meals.
<v Terry Smith>你在监狱里恐慌，你知道那会导致什么吗？那会带你去哪？它导致人们，噢，不。我们不去回我们的铺位了。我们没有——三天了，我们没有吃任何饭。

00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:02.200
<v Sean Cole>You didn't eat meals for three days?
<v Sean Cole>你们三天没吃饭了？

00:43:02.200 --> 00:43:03.170
<v Terry Smith>For three days.
<v Terry Smith>三天了。

00:43:03.170 --> 00:43:06.410
<v Sean Cole>Because you were worried about getting the virus?
<v Sean Cole>因为你们担心感染病毒？

00:43:06.410 --> 00:43:09.080
<v Terry Smith>No, well, because so, when they serve the meals, right?
<v Terry Smith>不，好吧，因为，当他们上餐时，对吧？

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:09.930
<v Sean Cole>Mm-hmm.
<v Sean Cole>嗯嗯。

00:43:09.930 --> 00:43:25.040
<v Terry Smith>So you have a guy who hands you an empty tray. That's one meal. And there's five people who put something on the tray, the beans, and then they put it into the jello, or the salad. So that's five people, including the guy who puts the tray on there, six people.
<v Terry Smith>所以有一个人递给你一个空托盘。那是一顿饭。有五个人往托盘上放东西，豆子，然后他们把它放进果冻或者沙拉里。所以那是五个人，加上那个递托盘的人，六个人。

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:36.170
<v Terry Smith>And then you've got the guy that takes the tray at the end and stacks it up and put it on a cart. Now we're at seven people, right? They're not wearing masks. They're wearing gloves, but they're not wearing masks.
<v Terry Smith>然后你还有最后取托盘、把它叠起来放到手推车上的人。现在是七个人了，对吧？他们没戴口罩。他们戴着手套，但没戴口罩。

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:36.940
<v Sean Cole>So you think--
<v Sean Cole>所以你认为——

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:37.740
<v Terry Smith>Also--
<v Terry Smith>还有——

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:40.800
<v Sean Cole>You think they're not taking enough precautions if they're in the jail.
<v Sean Cole>你认为如果他们在监狱里，就没有采取足够的预防措施。

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:48.200
<v Terry Smith>No, sir. I mean, they're doing the best they can. But I think they should-- those servers should be wearing masks.
<v Terry Smith>不，先生。我的意思是，他们在尽力。但我认为他们应该——那些送餐员应该戴口罩。

00:43:48.200 --> 00:43:59.700
<v Terry Smith>There's no hand sanitizer. So no one wants to eat the food. They're scared. A lot of older guys start trying to teach the young guys, dude, you need to like start washing your bed areas.
<v Terry Smith>没有洗手液。所以没人想吃那些食物。他们很害怕。许多年长的人开始教年轻人，伙计，你们需要像开始清洗你们的床铺区域一样。

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:10.830
<v Terry Smith>You need to start really not touching each other. You stop hand touching. You're not even fully aware of how to, really-- to address this mess.
<v Terry Smith>你们需要真正开始不相互接触。停止手触碰。你们甚至没有完全意识到如何，真正地——处理这团混乱。

00:44:10.830 --> 00:44:34.360
<v Sean Cole>Terry has served three years, not in prison, in jail, pre-trial, waiting for his case to be resolved. His lawyer, a guy named Eric Quandt, says that's the amount of time he should serve for the crimes he's accused of. And Eric has been filing motions, trying to get a settlement of some kind, maybe a plea deal. But it kept getting delayed and delayed. And then the virus hit.
<v Sean Cole>特里服刑了三年，不是在监狱，是在羁押室，审前羁押，等待案件解决。他的律师，一个叫埃里克·匡特（Eric Quandt）的人，说这是他为所指控的罪行应该服刑的时间。埃里克一直提交动议，试图达成某种和解，也许是认罪协议。但它一直被推迟又推迟。然后病毒袭来了。

00:44:34.360 --> 00:44:49.220
<v Terry Smith>I was scared. And yes, to be honest with you, I was scared. I was scared to get the virus and die up here. Eric saved my life by doing what he did for me. Eric saved my life. And the judge.
<v Terry Smith>我很害怕。是的，老实说，我很害怕。我害怕感染病毒死在这里。埃里克通过他为我所做的拯救了我的生命。埃里克拯救了我的生命。还有法官。

00:44:49.220 --> 00:44:54.440
<v Sean Cole>It just seems so crazy that it was a virus that, essentially, came to your rescue.
<v Sean Cole>病毒，本质上是来拯救你的，这看起来太疯狂了。

00:44:54.440 --> 00:44:56.560
<v Terry  Smith>Yeah, a virus.
<v Terry  Smith>是的，病毒。

00:44:56.560 --> 00:45:27.880
<v Sean Cole>That was all it took for the criminal justice system to kick into gear, a deadly scourge that's devastating the world. So Terry is no longer behind bars and walks out into a city that's completely on lockdown. Remember, San Francisco was one of the first cities to issue a shelter in place ordinance. He told me he had all these fantasies about what he would do when he was free again-- see his girlfriend. Say hi to his friends. Instead, he's not seeing anyone except the other guys in the facility.
<v Sean Cole>这就是刑事司法系统开始运转所需要的全部，一场蹂躏世界的致命灾难。所以特里不再被关在牢里，走进了完全处于封锁状态的城市。记住，旧金山是最早发布“居家避难”法令的城市之一。他告诉我，他曾幻想自己恢复自由后要做什么——见女朋友。跟朋友打招呼。相反，除了项目里的其他人，他谁也见不到。

00:45:27.880 --> 00:45:37.300
<v Terry Smith>My granddaughter's, we-- she's really close to me, real close, OK. I could wait. I'm getting out. I'm going to go get my baby and put her in my arms, you know what I mean?
<v Terry Smith>我的孙女，我们——她和我真的很亲，非常亲，好吧。我能等。我要出去了。我要去把我的宝贝抱进怀里，你知道我的意思吗？

00:45:37.300 --> 00:45:47.900
<v Terry Smith>So she says-- she don't understand. Papa, you're out. Why can't you come see me? I want to make sure that, when I come see you, I'm OK to come see you. I got to make sure I'm not bringing her nothing, you know what I mean?
<v Terry Smith>所以她说——她不明白。爸爸，你出来了。为什么你不能来看我？我想确保，当我来看你时，我身体健康。我必须确保我没有给她带去什么东西，你知道我的意思吗？

00:45:47.900 --> 00:46:07.160
<v Sean Cole>It's like these two opposite things, being incarcerated and not being incarcerated, inched ever so slightly closer to each other when Terry wasn't looking. His life kind of looks a lot like other people's in America right now, happening indoors, looking at the outside world like it's an exhibit. But listen to how he talks about it.
<v Sean Cole>就像这两种相反的事情，被监禁和没有被监禁，在特里没注意的时候，彼此稍微靠近了一点。他的生活现在看起来很像美国其他人的生活，发生在室内，把外面的世界看作是一个展品。但听听他是怎么说的。

00:46:07.160 --> 00:46:22.240
<v Terry Smith>I can look right now-- from the kitchen, I can see the beach. One block from me to the right, one block from me to the right is the park, Golden Gate Park. 10 blocks down, I can see the ocean. I see it every night.
<v Terry Smith>我现在能看到——从厨房里，我能看到海滩。离我右边一个街区，离我右边一个街区就是公园，金门公园。再往下走 10 个街区，我能看到海洋。我每天晚上都能看到。

00:46:22.240 --> 00:46:29.600
<v Terry Smith>I watch the sunset. I sat last night and watched the sunset-- red. So want to hear my breakfast? I had an avocado and banana.
<v Terry Smith>我看日落。我昨晚坐着看日落——红色的。所以想听听我的早餐吗？我吃了一个牛油果和一个香蕉。

00:46:29.600 --> 00:46:50.440
<v Sean Cole>It's the same words that a lot of us are saying to each other on the phone or Skype or whatever. This is what I'm looking at out my window. This is what I ate. But for Terry, they're a celebration.
<v Sean Cole>这些是我们在电话、Skype 或其他任何工具上对彼此说的同样的话。这是我从窗外看到的。这是我吃的。但对于特里来说，它们是一场庆祝。

00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:56.470
<v Ira Glass>Sean is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:46:56.470 --> 00:47:06.390
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "BORED" BY MNEK"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - MNEK 的《BORED》]

00:47:06.390 --> 00:47:42.880
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Hello From the Other Side-- OK, so Emanuele Berry, who you heard earlier in the show in act two-- Emanuele used to live in China. And she's been texting back and forth with friends there since January when the virus shut down parts of that country. Now, as American cities are shutting down, a lot of her friends in China are texting her, asking how she's doing. Here's Emanuele.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，来自另一边的问候——好的，你们在第二幕听到过的埃曼努埃尔·贝里——埃曼努埃尔曾经住在中国。自从一月病毒封锁了那个国家部分地区以来，她一直和那里的朋友发短信联系。现在，随着美国城市陆续封锁，她在中国的许多朋友都在发短信问她怎么样。这是埃曼努埃尔。

00:47:42.880 --> 00:47:54.300
<v Emanuele Berry>The other day, I got this text from China. It said, "A word from your future-- it will get better. Today, we were on the street, and it almost felt normal."
<v Emanuele Berry>前几天，我收到了这条来自中国的短信。它说：“来自你未来的一句话——情况会变好的。今天，我们在街上，感觉几乎正常了。”

00:47:54.300 --> 00:48:17.880
<v Emanuele Berry>It arrived during a hard week. I was worried about my dad, who's high risk, money, my friends who work in hospitals, my best friend who's supposed to have a kid any day now. I'm constantly thinking about how uncertain each day feels. And I'm craving normal. My friend Rebecca Kanthor, sent it. She lives in Shanghai. And I called her to hear more about this normal.
<v Emanuele Berry>它是在艰难的一周收到的。我担心我高风险的爸爸，担心钱，担心在医院工作的朋友，担心我最好的朋友，她随时都有可能生孩子。我一直在思考每一天是多么的不确定。我渴望正常。我的朋友丽贝卡·坎索尔（Rebecca Kanthor）发来的。她住在上海。我打电话给她了解更多关于这种“正常”的情况。

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:20.700
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>Oh, hey. I got to tell my husband to stop--
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>噢，嘿。我得告诉我丈夫别——

00:48:20.700 --> 00:48:21.540
<v Emanuele Berry>[LAUGHS]
<v Emanuele Berry>[笑声]

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:26.600
<v Rebecca Kanthor>He's like in the-- hey, yo. [NON-ENGLISH]. [WHISTLES]
<v Rebecca Kanthor>他就像在——嘿，哟。（中文）。（哨声）

00:48:26.600 --> 00:48:37.140
<v Emanuele Berry>Rebecca is American, but she's lived in China for 17 years. She's married to a Chinese man. Her kids go to Chinese schools.
<v Emanuele Berry>丽贝卡是美国人，但她在中国的居住了 17 年。她嫁给了一个中国男人。她的孩子们上中国的学校。

00:48:37.140 --> 00:48:41.720
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>He's carrying a hunk of frozen meat.
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>他正扛着一大块冷冻肉。

00:48:41.720 --> 00:48:42.010
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>[LAUGHTER]
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>[笑声]

00:48:42.010 --> 00:48:46.230
<v Emanuele Berry>Wait, for what? Is he cooking?
<v Emanuele Berry>等等，为了什么？他在做饭吗？

00:48:46.230 --> 00:48:49.950
<v Rebecca Kanthor>I think he's hungry, and he wants to cut a piece off of it.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>我想他饿了，他想切一块下来。

00:48:49.950 --> 00:49:08.870
<v Emanuele Berry>Husband out of the way, we talked. China, of course, has had a much more aggressive response to the virus than anything that's happened in the states so far. They locked down Wuhan, traced contacts, isolated people who are sick, sometimes separating them from their families. The government mandated quarantines.
<v Emanuele Berry>丈夫走开了，我们交谈起来。当然，中国对病毒的反应比目前在各州发生的任何事情都更加积极。他们封锁了武汉，追踪接触者，隔离病人，有时将他们与家人分离。政府强制实施隔离。

00:49:08.870 --> 00:49:28.350
<v Emanuele Berry>The governor of New York has suggested it might be four to nine months before a stay at home orders are lifted. But in Wuhan, just two months in, the city is set to open up again. And in cities like Beijing and Shanghai, the few new cases being reported are coming from travelers. They've been told to resume life as normal.
<v Emanuele Berry>纽约州长建议居家避难令解除可能需要四到九个月。但在武汉，仅仅两个月，该市准备重新开放了。而在北京和上海这样的城市，报告的少数新病例来自旅行者。他们被告知要恢复正常生活。

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:29.860
<v Rebecca Kanthor>[SIGHS] How are you doing?
<v Rebecca Kanthor>[叹气] 你怎么样？

00:49:29.860 --> 00:49:51.100
<v Emanuele  Berry>How am I doing? [LAUGHS] I don't know. Like, I think I've accepted that it's going to be tough. But part of me just wants to, like, know that it's going to be OK at the end of it, even if it's going to be so like-- if it's going to be difficult, if that makes sense, you know?
<v Emanuele  Berry>我怎么样？（笑声）我不知道。就像，我想我已经接受了它会很艰难。但我的内心只是想，就像，知道到最后它会没事的，即使它会很——如果它会很困难的话，如果这话听起来有道理的话，你知道吧？

00:49:51.100 --> 00:49:53.840
<v Rebecca Kanthor>You want to know it's going to be OK in the end?
<v Rebecca Kanthor>你想知道到最后一切都会好起来的？

00:49:53.840 --> 00:49:58.960
<v Emanuele Berry>I mean, what was it-- what was it like for you two months ago?
<v Emanuele Berry>我的意思是，两个月前对你们来说是什么样的？

00:49:58.960 --> 00:50:15.720
<v Rebecca Kanthor>Yeah, it's-- I mean, it definitely feels different now. I mean, two months ago, it just felt like, yeah, just so uncertain and things changing by the day. Yeah, so just every week was different.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>是的，它——我的意思是，现在感觉绝对不同了。我的意思是，两个月前，它就是感觉，是的，就是那么不确定，事情每天都在变。是的，所以每周都不同。

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:24.180
<v Emanuele Berry>At this point, you're, maybe, on the other side of this-- possibly on the other side of this, like what-- what does that look like?
<v Emanuele Berry>目前，你可能已经在这一切的另一面了——可能在另一面了，那——那看起来像什么？

00:50:24.180 --> 00:50:42.100
<v Rebecca Kanthor>Well, OK, so yeah, things are, you know, feeling much better here. I think it just feels like, you know, taking off like a really hot jacket. You know like when you're wearing a winter jacket and, all of a sudden, the weather gets warmer, and you're like, ah, I don't need to be wearing this anymore.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>嗯，好吧，所以是的，事情，你知道，在这里感觉好多了。我想这只是感觉，你知道，像脱掉一件非常热的夹克。你知道当你穿着冬装夹克，突然天气变暖了，你会说，啊，我不需要再穿这个了。

00:50:42.100 --> 00:50:49.100
<v Rebecca Kanthor>You take it off. And you've got short sleeve shirt-- short sleeves on. And you just feel just relaxed.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>你把它脱掉。你穿着短袖衬衫——短袖。你就感觉很放松。

00:50:49.100 --> 00:50:52.510
<v Emanuele Berry>Can you take the train now? Like, what are the trains like?
<v Emanuele Berry>你们现在能坐火车了吗？比如，火车是什么样的？

00:50:52.510 --> 00:51:03.990
<v Rebecca Kanthor>Yes, I just saw a picture from the train today. And it would-- at the beginning, the trains were-- the subways were still running, I think, the whole way through.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>是的，我今天刚看到一张火车的照片。它会——在开始时，火车——地铁仍然在运行，我想，一直到最后。

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:19.920
<v Rebecca Kanthor>But back in the beginning, there was like one or two people per car. And now, all the seats-- at least the photo I saw, all the seats were being sat in. But I haven't taken the subway since January.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>但在最初的时候，每节车厢里大概有一两个人。而现在，所有的座位——至少我看到的照片，所有的座位都坐满了人。但我从一月份开始就没坐过地铁了。

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:23.410
<v Emanuele Berry>You haven't? Is there-- is there a reason for that? Or is it just--
<v Emanuele Berry>你没有？有什么——有什么原因吗？还是只是——

00:51:23.410 --> 00:51:39.060
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>I think I just told myself that that was something that I could control. And I just thought, well, if I can get it there on my electric bike or my bicycle, then I'll go to those places. But if I-- I just--
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>我想我只是告诉自己那是我可以控制的事情。我只是想，嗯，如果我能骑电动自行车或自行车到达那里，那我就去那些地方。但如果我——我只是——

00:51:39.060 --> 00:51:49.250
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>I stopped taking taxis. And I stopped taking the subway. Even though they're all running, it's just kind of my personal thing that I decided I wasn't going to do.
<v Rebecca  Kanthor>我不再坐出租车了。我也不再坐地铁了。尽管它们都在运行，但这只是我个人决定的事情，我不打算做。

00:51:49.250 --> 00:51:59.820
<v Emanuele Berry>As I talked to Rebecca and other friends in China, I've noticed this thing. When they talk about things returning to normal, they start describing all these things that aren't normal.
<v Emanuele Berry>当我和丽贝卡以及其他在中国的朋友交谈时，我注意到了这件事。当他们谈论事情回归正常时，他们开始描述所有不正常的事情。

00:51:59.820 --> 00:52:02.980
<v Rebecca Kanthor>You know, everyone's still wearing a mask.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>你知道，每个人仍然戴着口罩。

00:52:02.980 --> 00:52:09.520
<v Emanuele Berry>Here, we're told not to wear a mask. In some cities in China, masks are mandatory.
<v Emanuele Berry>这里，我们被告知不要戴口罩。在中国的一些城市，口罩是强制性的。

00:52:09.520 --> 00:52:21.060
<v Rebecca Kanthor>Or like I saw-- like the other day, I saw someone taking a selfie. And then, like, halfway through the selfie, they were like, oh, shoot. I'm still wearing my mask. So they to remember, take it off, and then take the selfie again.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>或者就像我看到——就像前几天，我看到有人在自拍。然后，就像，自拍拍了一半，他们就像，噢，该死。我还在戴着口罩。所以他们得记住，脱下它，然后再拍一次自拍。

00:52:21.060 --> 00:52:29.600
<v Emanuele Berry>What-- does that mask-- does it sort of feel like this reminder to you that it's like, oh, this isn't normal?
<v Emanuele Berry>那——那个口罩——它是否感觉像是在提醒你，就像，噢，这不正常？

00:52:29.600 --> 00:52:53.760
<v Rebecca Kanthor>Yeah, yeah, it's kind of like-- because actually the government said you can take off your masks. They said-- you know, they had a press conference. And the government official took their mask off and was like, you can take your masks off. You only need them when you go into certain public areas. But in reality, most people are still wearing them.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>是的，是的，它有点像——因为实际上政府说你们可以脱下口罩了。他们说——你知道，他们召开了新闻发布会。政府官员摘下口罩说，你们可以脱掉口罩了。你们只需要在进入某些公共区域时才需要它们。但在现实中，大多数人仍然戴着它们。

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:59.670
<v Emanuele Berry>And it's not just that people are still wearing masks. Temperature taking is now everywhere.
<v Emanuele Berry>而且不仅仅是人们仍然戴着口罩。测体温现在到处都是。

00:52:59.670 --> 00:53:22.100
<v Rebecca Kanthor>So when you go into any public space and also when you enter any housing compound, like an apartment complex, there's someone at the gate with a little body sensor thermometer. And they put it up to your head, or they put it on your wrist, and take your temperature.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>所以当你进入任何公共空间，以及当你进入任何住宅区，比如公寓大楼时，门口有人拿着一个小身体传感器温度计。他们把它贴到你的头上，或者把它放到你的手腕上，测量你的体温。

00:53:22.100 --> 00:53:35.920
<v Emanuele Berry>The newest thing for Rebecca to get used to has been this QR code system. It basically sorts people. Should you be quarantined or not? It's a little unclear how people are being sorted into these categories.
<v Emanuele Berry>丽贝卡需要习惯的最新事物是这个二维码系统。它基本上将人们分类。你应该被隔离吗？人们是如何被分类到这些类别中还不是很清楚。

00:53:35.920 --> 00:53:50.360
<v Emanuele Berry>But it involves people's locations being tracked through their cell phone. If you've recently visited a hospital, been around a sick person, bought a train or a plane ticket, it's being monitored. The code is like an admission ticket to do anything in the city.
<v Emanuele Berry>但它涉及人们的位置通过手机进行追踪。如果你最近访问过医院，在病人身边待过，买过火车票或飞机票，它都在被监控。二维码就像在城市做任何事情的入场券。

00:53:50.360 --> 00:54:13.810
<v Rebecca Kanthor>I went to visit a friend at their apartment complex. And I had to-- first of all, I had to register my name, phone number, passport number. And then I also had to show them on my phone a-- like a QR code, like a little square code that shows that I've been in Shanghai for the past 14 days. So I get a green code.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>我去参观朋友的公寓大楼。我不得不——首先，我必须登记我的姓名、电话号码、护照号码。然后我还必须在我的手机上出示一个——就像一个二维码，就像一个小方块代码，显示我在过去 14 天里一直待在上海。所以我得到了一个绿色代码。

00:54:13.810 --> 00:54:29.590
<v Rebecca Kanthor>And you know, the only way they can find that out is they have my phone records and like my GPS coordinates or whatever. I don't know how they find it. But that's not something that-- I don't know. Is that possible in the US?
<v Rebecca Kanthor>而且你知道，他们能找到这一点的唯一方法就是他们有我的电话记录和我之类 GPS 坐标。我不知道他们是怎么找到的。但那不是——我不知道。这在美国可能吗？

00:54:29.590 --> 00:54:31.920
<v Emanuele Berry>I don't-- I don't think-- I don't think so. [LAUGHS]
<v Emanuele Berry>我——我不认为——我不认为如此。（笑声）

00:54:31.920 --> 00:54:55.970
<v Rebecca Kanthor>Right, now, that offers-- that gives me-- that makes me feel like I have a sense of security, to be honest, because I look at it and I say, oh, well, I've been here for the past 14 days. I'm in the safe zone. You know, and if you go elsewhere, if you, maybe, go to another city or you travel abroad and you come back, then you're in a yellow zone or a red zone.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>没错，现在，那提供——那给予我——那让我觉得我有了一种安全感，说实话，因为我看着它说，噢，好吧，我在过去 14 天里一直待在这里。我在安全区。你知道，如果你去别处，如果你可能去另一个城市，或者你出国旅行又回来，那么你就在黄色区域或红色区域了。

00:54:55.970 --> 00:55:03.650
<v Emanuele Berry>To play the game of basketball, you need a QR code. And then when you get to the park, a volunteer takes your temperature before letting you in.
<v Emanuele Berry>要打篮球，你需要一个二维码。当你到达公园时，志愿者在让你进去之前会先测体温。

00:55:03.650 --> 00:55:25.500
<v Rebecca Kanthor>They do limit the numbers of people going into the park across the street from my house. But there's just-- it's always lots of people there now. But I think they still-- I do not think they have let the ladies do their square dancing at night. I think that is not back to normal.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>他们确实限制了进入我家对面公园的人数。但那里只是——现在总是很多人。但我认为他们仍然——我不认为他们让女士们晚上跳广场舞了。我想那还没恢复正常。

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:40.380
<v Rebecca Kanthor>So usually, at nighttime, like around 7:00 PM in all the parks, you'll have like older women and older men doing dance routines, line dancing routines. And that has stopped. And I don't think it's come back.
<v Rebecca Kanthor>所以通常在晚上，比如晚上 7 点，在所有的公园里，会有像老太太和老大爷跳舞蹈套路，排舞套路。而那已经停止了。我想它还没回来。

00:55:40.380 --> 00:56:36.810
<v Emanuele Berry>Rebecca says they discourage it because the crowds would be too much. I doubt that our future in the US looks anything like what Rebecca is describing. But everything feels so chaotic here. And the idea that eventually that feeling stops, you get used to the new normal, I can take comfort in that.
<v Emanuele Berry>丽贝卡说他们不鼓励它，因为人群会太多。我怀疑我们在美国的未来是否看起来像丽贝卡描述的那样。但这里的一切感觉如此混乱。而那种感觉最终会停止的想法，你习惯了新常态，我能从中得到安慰。

00:56:36.810 --> 00:56:45.270
<v Ira Glass>Emanuele Berry.
<v Ira Glass>埃曼努埃尔·贝里。

